Add the layer **before** the first symbol (label) layer in the style instead of appending it at the end.  
`map.addLayer()` accepts a second argument – the id of the layer that should stay **above** the new one.  
If you pass the id of the first label layer, your data will be drawn underneath all street-name symbols.

```js
// 1️⃣ Find the first symbol layer (the one that draws labels)
let firstLabelId = null;
for (const layer of map.getStyle().layers) {
  if (layer.type === 'symbol') {
    firstLabelId = layer.id;
    break;               // stop at the first symbol layer
  }
}

// 2️⃣ Add your GeoJSON source (if you haven’t already)
map.addSource('my-data', {
  type: 'geojson',
  data: myGeojson
});

// 3️⃣ Add the layer, inserting it below the labels
map.addLayer(
  {
    id: 'my-data-fill',
    type: 'fill',               // or line, circle, etc.
    source: 'my-data',
    paint: {
      'fill-color': '#ff6600',
      'fill-opacity': 0.4
    }
  },
  firstLabelId   // ← second argument: insert below this layer
);
```

### Why this works
* Layers are rendered **bottom-to-top** in the order they appear in the style.
* When you call `map.addLayer()` with only one argument, the layer is appended **above everything**, which hides the basemap’s label symbols.
* Supplying the id of the first `symbol` layer tells MapLibre to place your new layer **just beneath** all subsequent symbol layers, preserving the street names and other labels.

If you know the exact label-layer id from the style (e.g., `"road-label"` or `"place-label"`), you can pass that string directly instead of searching for the first symbol layer. The programmatic search above works for any style, even when the label-layer ids differ.

You’re almost there – the source definition itself is fine, but a classic OSM tile service serves **256 px tiles**, while MapLibre’s raster source defaults to **512 px**. That makes the imagery appear at the wrong zoom level (it looks “off-scale” or disappears as you zoom).

Add `tileSize: 256` (and, optionally, the usual metadata) and then add a raster layer that points at the source:

```js
// 1️⃣  Raster source
map.addSource('osm-basemap', {
  type: 'raster',
  tiles: ['https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'],
  tileSize: 256,          // <-- required for OSM tiles
  minzoom: 0,
  maxzoom: 19,            // OSM’s published range
  attribution: '© OpenStreetMap contributors'
});

// 2️⃣  Raster layer (inserted below the first symbol layer so labels stay visible)
const firstSymbol = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol')?.id;
map.addLayer(
  {
    id: 'osm-basemap-layer',
    type: 'raster',
    source: 'osm-basemap',
    paint: {}
  },
  firstSymbol   // insert below labels; omit to append on top
);
```

**What this does**

| Piece | Why it matters |
|------|----------------|
| `tileSize: 256` | Aligns MapLibre’s tile-size expectation with OSM’s 256 px tiles; otherwise the map looks zoomed out or tiles vanish. |
| `minzoom` / `maxzoom` | Gives MapLibre the correct zoom range; OSM serves 0-19. |
| `attribution` | Required by OSM’s license and shows up in the map’s attribution control. |
| Inserting the layer **before** the first `symbol` layer | Prevents your raster from covering street-name labels and other symbols. |

OSM’s tile server already sends the proper CORS headers, so no extra CORS configuration is needed. With the source, `tileSize`, and layer added as shown, the tiles will render correctly.

No. `mapbox://styles/...` URLs are **style URLs**, not tile-source URLs, and MapLibre GL JS does not understand them as a source definition.

A MapLibre source’s `url` field must point at a **TileJSON document** (or a raw tile-template array in `tiles`). The `mapbox://styles/…` URL returns a full style JSON (layers, paint, layout, etc.) and requires a Mapbox access token; it isn’t a TileJSON endpoint that describes a vector-tile source.

If you want to use the same data that the Mapbox “Streets-v11” style draws, you have two practical options:

1. **Load the whole style directly**  
   ```js
   const map = new maplibregl.Map({
     container: 'map',
     style: 'https://api.mapbox.com/styles/v1/mapbox/streets-v11?access_token=YOUR_TOKEN',
     // …
   });
   ```
   This works because MapLibre can fetch the style JSON (provided you supply a valid token and the request passes CORS). It’s still a *style*, not a source you add yourself.

2. **Use the underlying vector-tile source**  
   Find the TileJSON endpoint for the vector tiles that the style uses (e.g. the Mapbox “streets” tileset) and reference that in a source definition:

   ```js
   map.addSource('streets', {
     type: 'vector',
     url: 'https://api.mapbox.com/v4/mapbox.mapbox-streets-v8.json?access_token=YOUR_TOKEN'
   });
   ```

   Then you can add your own layers that reference `source: 'streets'` and the appropriate `source-layer` names.

In short: you cannot drop a `mapbox://styles/...` URL into a source’s `url` field. Use either the full style URL as the map’s `style` property, or locate the TileJSON URL for the vector tiles you need and use that as the source. Remember to include a valid Mapbox access token and ensure CORS headers are present on the TileJSON endpoint.

`setFeatureState` works only on features that have an **id** that MapLibre can see.  
Your parcels are coming from a **GeoJSON** source, and the id lives inside the
`properties` object as `parcel_id`.  
MapLibre does not look inside `properties` unless you tell it which property to
promote to the feature id.

Add `promoteId: 'parcel_id'` (or an object if you have multiple source-layers)
when you create the source, then call `setFeatureState` with that same value.

```js
// 1️⃣  Create the source – promote the parcel_id property to the feature id
map.addSource('parcels', {
  type: 'geojson',
  data: parcelsGeojson,          // your FeatureCollection
  promoteId: 'parcel_id'        // <-- the missing piece
});

// 2️⃣  Add a layer that uses the feature-state expression
map.addLayer({
  id: 'parcels-fill',
  type: 'fill',
  source: 'parcels',
  paint: {
    // default fill
    'fill-color': '#88c0d0',
    // when the feature’s state.hover === true, use a brighter colour
    'fill-color': [
      'case',
      ['boolean', ['feature-state', 'hover'], false],
      '#ffcc00',               // highlighted colour
      '#88c0d0'                // normal colour
    ]
  }
});

// 3️⃣  On mouse move (or click) set the state
let hoveredId = null;

map.on('mousemove', 'parcels-fill', e => {
  if (!e.features.length) return;

  const feature = e.features[0];
  const id = feature.id;               // <-- now contains parcel_id

  if (hoveredId !== null && hoveredId !== id) {
    // clear previous hover
    map.setFeatureState({ source: 'parcels', id: hoveredId }, { hover: false });
  }

  // set hover on the new feature
  map.setFeatureState({ source: 'parcels', id }, { hover: true });
  hoveredId = id;
});

// 4️⃣  Clean up when the mouse leaves the layer
map.on('mouseleave', 'parcels-fill', () => {
  if (hoveredId !== null) {
    map.setFeatureState({ source: 'parcels', id: hoveredId }, { hover: false });
    hoveredId = null;
  }
});
```

### Why this fixes the problem

| What was missing | What MapLibre needed |
|------------------|----------------------|
| **Feature id**   | `setFeatureState` attaches state to a feature **by its id**. Without an id the call is a no-op and `getFeatureState` returns `{}`. |
| **`promoteId`**  | Tells MapLibre to treat the `parcel_id` property as the feature’s id, making it available as `feature.id` in the layer and as the `id` argument to `setFeatureState`. |
| **Correct id type** | `parcel_id` can be a string or number; `promoteId` works with either. No need for `generateId` or manually adding a top-level `id`. |

After adding `promoteId`, the hover state will be stored, the paint expression will evaluate, and `map.getFeatureState({ source: 'parcels', id })` will return the `{ hover: true }` object you set.
